<h2>Glossary</h2>
<div id=3D"results">
<dl>
<dt class=3D"first" id=3D"reference_accent">accent:</dt>
<dd class=3D"first">
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">emphasis given a syllable in ordinary usage, as prov=
ided by a pronouncing dictionary.<span> </span>See also <a href=3D"https://=
prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_stress">stress</a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_accentual-syllabic">accentual-syllabic:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_pros=
ody"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">prosodic</span></a> mode t=
hat dominated English-language poetry 1400-1900, and that this tutorial exc=
lusively addresses.  Alike distinct from verse that is <a href=3D"https://p=
rosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_quantity"><span style=3D"text-d=
ecoration: underline;">quantitative</span></a> (measuring duration, as in c=
lassical Greek and Latin), accentual (counting only beats, as in Old Englis=
h), and syllabic (counting only syllables, as in certain: 20th-cy. experime=
nts), accentual-syllabic verse is based on recurrent units (<a href=3D"http=
s://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_foot"><span style=3D"text-=
decoration: underline;">feet</span></a>) that combine slacks and stresses i=
n fixed sequence.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_acephalous-line">acephalous line:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a =E2=80=9Cheadless=E2=80=9D line in iambic or anapestic meter, which om=
its (a) slack syllable(s) from the first foot.   See also <a href=3D"https:=
//prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_catalectic-line">catalectic =
line</a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_alexandrine">alexandrine:</dt>
<dd>
<p>iambic hexameter line, usually with a strong midpoint <a href=3D"https:/=
/prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_caesura"><span style=3D"text-=
decoration: underline;">caesura</span></a>; most familiar in Romance-langua=
ge poetry but not rare in English.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_alliteration">alliteration:</dt>
<dd>
<p>repetition of the same initial sound in nearby words.  See also <a href=
=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_consonance"><span =
style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">consonance</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_anapest">anapest:</dt>
<dd>
<p>metrical foot consisting of two slacks and a stress:  =CF=85  =CF=85  /<=
/p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_anaphora">anaphora:</dt>
<dd>
<p>repetition of a word or phrase in initial position.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_assonance">assonance:</dt>
<dd>
<p>harmonious repetition of the same vowel sound in nearby words.  See also=
 <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_consonance=
"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">consonance</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_ballad-meter">ballad meter:</dt>
<dd>
<p><a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_quatrain=
"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">quatrain</span> </a>in altern=
ating iambic tetrameter and trimeter lines rhyming <em>abxb</em>, tradition=
ally used in folk narrative and during modern times adapted to lyric poetry=
.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_blank-verse">blank verse:</dt>
<dd>
<p>unrhymed iambic pentameter.  See for contrast <a href=3D"https://prosody=
.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_free-verse"><span style=3D"text-decor=
ation: underline;">free verse</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_caesura">caesura:</dt>
<dd>
<p>mid-line pause, often marked by punctuation but not always. See also <a =
href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_hemistich"><sp=
an style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">hemistich</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_catalectic-line">catalectic line:</dt>
<dd>
<p>line lacking one or more of the slack syllables that its meter strictly =
followed would specify.  Also called =E2=80=9Cdefective.=E2=80=9D  See <a h=
ref=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_acephalous-line=
">acephalous line,</a> <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary=
/#reference_terminal-truncation"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;=
">terminal truncation</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_common-measure">common measure:</dt>
<dd>
<p>tetrameter <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#referen=
ce_quatrain"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">quatrain </span></=
a>rhyming <em>abab. </em>Also called =E2=80=9Chymn stanza.=E2=80=9D</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_consonance">consonance:</dt>
<dd>
<p>repetition of the same consonant sound in nearby words; also called =E2=
=80=9Ceuphony.=E2=80=9D  See also <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.e=
du/glossary/#reference_alliteration"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underl=
ine;">alliteration</span></a><a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/gl=
ossary/#reference_dissonance"></a>, <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia=
.edu/glossary/#reference_assonance"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underli=
ne;">assonance</span></a>, <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glos=
sary/#reference_dissonance"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">dis=
sonance</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_couplet">couplet:</dt>
<dd>
<p>tersest of rhyming forms, a 2-line pattern of verse, whether free-standi=
ng (<em>aa</em>), gathered into stanzas (e.g., <em>aabbcc</em>), or extende=
d at great length in narrative or deliberative poetry: <em>aabbccddeeff. . =
. </em> .=E2=80=9D   =E2=80=9COpen couplets=E2=80=9D make free use of <a hr=
ef=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_enjambed_line"><=
span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">enjambed lines</span></a>; =E2=
=80=9Cclosed couplets=E2=80=9D are end-stopped, and are called =E2=80=9Cher=
oic couplets=E2=80=9D when in iambic pentameter.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_dactyl">dactyl:</dt>
<dd>
<p>metrical foot consisting of a stress and two slacks:   <strong>/  =CF=85=
  =CF=85</strong></p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_dissonance">dissonance:</dt>
<dd>
<p>jam of inharmonious word sounds; also called =E2=80=9Ccacophony.=E2=80=
=9D  See also <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#referen=
ce_consonance"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">consonance</span=
></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_elision">elision:</dt>
<dd>
<p>slurring of two syllables into one, across adjacent vowels or weak voice=
d consonants; includes conventional poeticisms (=E2=80=9Co=E2=80=99er=E2=80=
=9D for =E2=80=9Cover,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Ce=E2=80=99en=E2=80=9D for =E2=80=
=9Ceven=E2=80=9D) and  nonce phrases so marked (=E2=80=9Cth=E2=80=99 unseen=
=E2=80=9D) or so treated in scansion, usually to contract an anapest into a=
n iamb.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_enjambed_line">enjambed line:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a line of verse whose sense runs on, without terminal punctuation, into =
the next.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_epigram">epigram:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a very short poem marked by pithy statement and witty turn.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_feminine-ending">feminine ending:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a line ending whose last syllable is a slack; normal in trochaic and dac=
tylic verse, it also frequently arises in anapestic and even iambic verse. =
 =E2=80=9CFeminine rhyme=E2=80=9D occurs when like sounds appear in a line=
=E2=80=99s last stress and the succeeding slacks are repeated exactly, e.g.=
 =E2=80=9Cgravity=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Csuavity.=E2=80=9D  See also <a href=
=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_masculine-ending">=
<span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">masculine ending</span></a>.</p=
>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_fixed-forms">fixed forms:</dt>
<dd>
<p>strictly set patterns of meter and/or rhyme or word recurrence.  Some, s=
uch as the ballade and roundeau, sestina and Petrarchan sonnet, are centuri=
es old; others are of recent invention or adoption, e.g. the clerihew, lime=
rick, roundel, villanelle.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_foot">foot:</dt>
<dd>
<p>repeating pattern of <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossar=
y/#reference_slack"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">slack </spa=
n></a>and <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_s=
tress"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">stressed </span></a>syll=
ables that forms the fundamental unit of <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.vir=
ginia.edu/glossary/#reference_meter"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underl=
ine;">meter</span></a>.  The number of feet in a line gives their names to =
monometer (1),  dimeter (2),  trimeter (3), tetrameter (4), pentameter (5),=
 hexameter (6, also called <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glos=
sary/#reference_alexandrine"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">al=
exandrine</span></a>), heptameter (7, also called =E2=80=9Cfourteeners=E2=
=80=9D), octometer (8), nonometer (9), the very first and the last two bein=
g quite rare.  See also <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossar=
y/#reference_scansion"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">scansion=
</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_free-verse">free verse:</dt>
<dd>
<p>unmetered poetry, often exhibiting great rhythmic power and finesse in f=
ormal lineation, but outside the scope of this tutorial on metrics.  See al=
so <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_blank-ve=
rse"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">blank verse</span></a>.</p=
>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_hemistich">hemistich:</dt>
<dd>
<p>part of a line on either side of a <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virgin=
ia.edu/glossary/#reference_caesura"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underli=
ne;">caesura</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_iamb">iamb:</dt>
<dd>
<p>metrical foot consisting of a slack and a stress:   <strong>=CF=85  /</s=
trong></p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_masculine-ending">masculine ending:</dt>
<dd>
<p>line ending where the last syllable is stressed.  See also <a href=3D"ht=
tps://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_feminine-ending"><span s=
tyle=3D"text-decoration: underline;">feminine ending</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_meter">meter:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the recurring, invariant pattern of <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virgi=
nia.edu/glossary/#reference_sla"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;=
">slack </span></a>and <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary=
/#reference_stress"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">stressed </=
span></a>syllables that a line of accentual-syllabic verse implies, and tha=
t the reader infers as an abstract, steady ground against which the actual =
verse <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_r"><s=
pan style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">rhythm </span></a>plays its patte=
rns of coincidence or counterpoint.  See also <a href=3D"https://prosody.li=
b.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_foot"><span style=3D"text-decoration: un=
derline;">foot</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_octave">octave:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the first eight lines of a <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/g=
lossary/#reference_sonnet"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">sonn=
et</span></a>, almost always a complete rhyming unit.  See also <a href=3D"=
https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_sestet"><span style=3D=
"text-decoration: underline;">sestet</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_onomatopoeia">onomatopoeia:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the imitation of a sound by the word that denotes it.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_ottava-rima">ottava rima:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a stanza rhyming <em>abababcc.</em></p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_pitch">pitch:</dt>
<dd>
<p>high or low quality of sounds in a syllable: a property of both consonan=
ts and vowels, it is one contributing factor in the determination of stress=
.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_promoted-stress">promoted stress:</dt>
<dd>
<p>stress laid, in deference to the metrical pattern, on a word or syllable=
 that would in ordinary speech be slack.  Rare in polysyllables since 18th =
century.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_prosody">prosody:</dt>
<dd>
<p>study or practice or study of versification: what this tutorial is all a=
bout.  Also called =E2=80=9Cmetrics=E2=80=9D in the case of <a href=3D"http=
s://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_accentual-syllabic"><span =
style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">accentual-syllabic</span></a> poetry.=
</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_pyrrhic">pyrrhic:</dt>
<dd>
<p>metrical foot consisting of two consecutive slacks:  <strong>=CF=85 =CF=
=85 </strong>.  Occurs only in conjunction with its complementary foot the =
<a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_spondee"><s=
pan style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">spondee</span></a>, which assumes=
 or borrows its pyrrhic partner=E2=80=99s missing beat.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_quantity">quantity:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a metrical principle of Greek and Latin <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.v=
irginia.edu/glossary/#reference_prosody"><span style=3D"text-decoration: un=
derline;">prosody</span></a> tied to the length of syllables spoken or chan=
ted.  In antiquity the permutation of long with short quantities gave rise =
to a veritable foot fetishism, whose taxonomy of terms from amphibrach to s=
yzygy leaves this poor glossary in the dust.  The good news for us is that =
quantity proves practically useless for the analysis of <a href=3D"https://=
prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_accentual-syllabic"><span styl=
e=3D"text-decoration: underline;">accentual-syllabic</span></a> verse.  Eve=
n where poets in English deliberately imitate such classical forms as alcai=
cs and sapphics, they rely on stresses and slacks, not longs and shorts.</p=
>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_quatrain">quatrain:</dt>
<dd>
<p>four-line stanza.  Variants include, in pentameter, the =E2=80=9Cheroic=
=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Celegiac quatrain=E2=80=9D (<em>abab</em>) and =E2=80=
=9C<em>Rub=C3=A1iy=C3=A1t</em> quatrain=E2=80=9D (<em>aaxa</em>); in tetram=
eter, the <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_b=
allad-meter"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">ballad</span></a>,=
 the <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_common=
-measure"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">common<strong> </stro=
ng>measure</span></a>, the <em>In Memoriam</em> stanza (<em>abba</em>), and=
 the =E2=80=9Cshort measure=E2=80=9D (<em>abab</em>, line 3 being pentamete=
r).</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_quintain">quintain:</dt>
<dd>
<p>five-line stanza; forms include the cinquain and limerick.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_refrain">refrain:</dt>
<dd>
<p>a line of verse that is repeated verbatim within or at the end of a stan=
za.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_rhyme">rhyme:</dt>
<dd>
<p>recurrence of like sounds in like verse position, especially the last st=
ressed syllable of a line.  In =E2=80=9Cfull=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cperfect=
=E2=80=9D rhyme all sounds but the first are repeated exactly; =E2=80=9Cide=
ntical=E2=80=9D rhyme (e.g., =E2=80=9Cgoon=E2=80=9D with =E2=80=9Clagoon=E2=
=80=9D or =E2=80=9Csee=E2=80=9D with =E2=80=9Csea=E2=80=9D) is rare, and so=
unds odd.  Rhymes where sounds match only approximately (e.g., =E2=80=9Cgoo=
n=E2=80=9D with =E2=80=9Cson=E2=80=9D) are variously called =E2=80=9Chalf,=
=E2=80=9D  =E2=80=9Cimperfect,=E2=80=9D  =E2=80=9Cnear,=E2=80=9D  =E2=80=9C=
slant.=E2=80=9D  =E2=80=9CLeonine=E2=80=9D rhyme couples a line=E2=80=99s m=
iddle syllable with its last; =E2=80=9Cinternal=E2=80=9D rhyme may be more =
loosely distributed.  =E2=80=9CEye=E2=80=9D rhyme (e.g., =E2=80=9Cbough=E2=
=80=9D with =E2=80=9Crough=E2=80=9D) privileges spelling over sound, though=
 in some cases the spelling commemorates a long-lost acoustic resemblance.<=
/p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_rhythm">rhythm:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the pattern of <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#ref=
erence_slack"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">slack </span></a>=
and <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_stress"=
><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">stress </span></a>that emerges=
 from a poem as actually voiced, or imaginatively heard.  Rhythm implies th=
e poem=E2=80=99s metrical norm, which it both generally observes and (as a =
rule) tactically disobeys here and there.  See also <a href=3D"https://pros=
ody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_m"><span style=3D"text-decoration:=
 underline;">meter</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_scansion">scansion:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the chief object of this on-line tutorial: an analytic process of mappin=
g the convergence and divergence (reinforcement and counterpoint) between t=
he <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_meter"><=
span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">meter </span></a>of verse and it=
s <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_rhythm"><=
span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">rhythm</span></a>.  A poem is sc=
anned by marking its <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#=
reference_stress"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">stressed </sp=
an></a>and <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_=
slack"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">slack </span></a>syllabl=
es and dividing them into <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/gloss=
ary/#reference_foot"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">feet</span=
></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_sestet">sestet:</dt>
<dd>
<p>the last six lines of a Petrarchan <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virgin=
ia.edu/glossary/#reference_sonnet"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underlin=
e;">sonnet</span></a>; often subtly discernible by tone or mood in a Shakes=
pearean sonnet as well, although less distinctly marked there by rhyme sche=
me.  See also <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#referen=
ce_octave"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">octave</span></a>.</=
p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_slack">slack:</dt>
<dd>
<p>unstressed syllable in metered verse; scansion mark =E2=88=AA</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_sonnet">sonnet:</dt>
<dd>
<p>poem of 14 lines usually in iambic pentameter and subdivided by one of t=
wo rhyme schemes: =E2=80=9CPetrarchan=E2=80=9D (or =E2=80=9CItalian=E2=80=
=9D) <em>abbaabba cdecde</em> and =E2=80=9CEnglish=E2=80=9D (or =E2=80=9CSh=
akespearean=E2=80=9D) <em>abab cdcd efef gg</em>.  See also <a href=3D"http=
s://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_octave">octave</a>, <a hre=
f=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_sestet"><span sty=
le=3D"text-decoration: underline;">sestet</span></a>, <a href=3D"https://pr=
osody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_volta"><span style=3D"text-decor=
ation: underline;">volta</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_spenserian-stanza">Spenserian stanza:</dt>
<dd>
<p>8 lines of iambic pentameter followed by a 9<sup>th</sup> hexameter line=
 (<a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_alexandri=
ne"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">alexandrine</span></a>), rh=
yming <em>ababbcbcc</em>; named for the Elizabethan poet who inaugurated it=
 in his romance epic <em>The Faerie Queene.</em></p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_spondee">spondee:</dt>
<dd>
<p>metrical foot consisting of two consecutive stresses:  <strong>/  / </st=
rong>.  Often appears in conjunction with its complementary foot the <a hre=
f=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_pyrrhic"><span st=
yle=3D"text-decoration: underline;">pyrrhic</span></a>, whose missing beat =
it borrows; but often arises independently, as well, to freight a line or r=
etard it.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_stanza">stanza:</dt>
<dd>
<p>group of lines whose meter and rhyme scheme follow a pattern that is exa=
ctly repeated, constituting the structural unit of a stanzaic poem.   See a=
lso <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_strophe=
"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">strophe</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_stress">stress:</dt>
<dd>
<p>emphasis that a syllable receives in metered verse; scansion mark  <stro=
ng>  =E2=81=84 </strong> Stress may be due to <a href=3D"https://prosody.li=
b.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_accent"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
underline;">accent<strong> </strong></span></a>(as given in dictionaries fo=
r polysyllabic words), to grammar (monosyllabic nouns and adjectives, e.g.,=
 routinely taking stress), to context (where a word acquires dramatic or rh=
etorical force), or, infrequently, to the exigency of <a href=3D"https://pr=
osody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_meter"><span style=3D"text-decor=
ation: underline;">meter</span></a> itself.  See also <a href=3D"https://pr=
osody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_slack"><span style=3D"text-decor=
ation: underline;">slack</span></a>, <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virgini=
a.edu/glossary/#reference_promoted-stress"><span style=3D"text-decoration: =
underline;">promoted stress</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_strophe">strophe:</dt>
<dd>
<p>group of lines forming a typographically discrete portion of a longer po=
em in irregularly rhymed, coupleted, or blank verse; also called =E2=80=9Cv=
erse paragraph.=E2=80=9D  See also <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.=
edu/glossary/#reference_stanza"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;"=
>stanza</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_tercet">tercet:</dt>
<dd>
<p>3-line pattern of verses, usually <em>aaa bbb. . . </em>when rhymed, but=
 common in free verse as well; also called =E2=80=9Ctriplet.=E2=80=9D</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_terminal-truncation">terminal truncation:</dt>
<dd>
<p>omission, at the end of a trochaic or dactylic line, of (a) slack syllab=
le(s) that the meter strictly followed would specify.  See also <a href=3D"=
https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#reference_catalectic-line"><span=
 style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">catalectic line</span></a>.</p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_terza-rima">terza rima:</dt>
<dd>
<p>sequence of <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#refere=
nce_tercet"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">tercets</span></a>,=
 as devised for Dante=E2=80=99s <em>Divina Commedia</em>,<em> </em>with int=
erlocking rhyme scheme <em>aba bcb cdc ded. . . .</em></p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_trochee">trochee:</dt>
<dd>
<p>metrical foot consisting of a stress and a slack: <strong>/ =CF=85 </str=
ong></p>
</dd>
<dt id=3D"reference_volta">volta:</dt>
<dd>
<p>pivot point of a <a href=3D"https://prosody.lib.virginia.edu/glossary/#r=
eference_sonnet"><span style=3D"text-decoration: underline;">sonnet</span><=
/a>, usually at or near the beginning of the 9<sup>th</sup> line.</p>
</dd>
</dl></div>
</div>
